      read: "This is the cleanest single piece of new evidence on the board today and it comes from the primary source rather than a summary. The minutes of the unanimous September 29 hike to 4.60% were published this morning on rba.gov.au and four passages matter. Members 'expressed their concern that excess demand was greater than previously assessed', having taken liaison, the July inflation data and resilient GDP and labour demand as pointing the same way. They saw 'an increase in the risk of persistently elevated inflation', attributing it to global cost pressures - specifically AI demand and higher oil prices - alongside firms' cost pressures and rising short-term inflation expectations. They 'briefly considered the arguments in support of leaving the cash rate target unchanged', resting that case on falling housing prices, Middle East uncertainty and the lagged effects of earlier tightening, and rejected it because scenario analysis suggested those effects would be modest. And they closed ready to act again 'including increasing the cash rate target further if required'. Two further lines are directly tradeable. Members noted that a higher global neutral rate could make policy less restrictive than it had been, which is a case for a higher terminal rate rather than a longer pause. And they noted that tightening abroad could weaken the Australian dollar and loosen conditions here - which, on a morning when December Fed pricing has jumped to 82-90%, is an argument that the RBA may have to do more rather than less. The forecasters have moved with the text: ANZ's Adam Boyton has a November increase as more likely than not and Westpac carries the cash rate to 4.85% by December, against CBA seeing a higher bar for further tightening. The supporting data is unchanged and still split. August headline CPI is 4.0% from 3.5% with the trimmed mean the Bank actually weighs held at 3.6% for a third month; unemployment is 4.6% and annual growth 2.1%. The terms of trade remain two-sided: copper at 6.6943 USD/lb is plus 1.51% on the week, plus 5.76% on the month, plus 17.82% year to date and plus 34.63% over twelve months, while iron ore at 90.83 USD/T - the larger export - is minus 6.89% on the month and minus 15.22% year to date. The honest caveat on this upgrade is that the price has not confirmed it: AUD/USD at 0.69605 is plus 0.02% on the day but minus 0.32% on the week and minus 2.52% on the month, holding a year-to-date gain of plus 4.31% that is still the only positive major against the dollar. The ten-year is 5.3000%, down about 2.7bp. Dated risks come fast: China September CPI on October 14, where a miss hits the currency and copper together, then September employment as a red folder at 00:30 UTC on October 15 forecast at plus 19.6K with unemployment at 4.6%, then Q3 CPI on October 28 six days before the meeting, which is what actually decides November 3."
