      read: "Yesterday's cut was reasoned off a number that has since moved against it, and the specific fact that changed is the crude price. The argument was that WTI at 90.409 sat exactly on the Bank of Canada's own baseline - crude falling toward about 75 dollars by mid-2027 from roughly 90 in the second quarter of 2026 - so the hawkish energy case was gone and the roughly 27% hike odds in the market were residue from a higher oil price. Crude then settled at 93.74 on Monday with Brent at 106.65, Brent's highest since September 15, and this morning WTI is 92.715 at minus 1.09% and Brent 105.585 at minus 1.00%, leaving crude plus 3.66% on the week and Brent plus 4.98%. That puts the price above the Bank's assumed path rather than on it. Macklem's September 21 framing in Halifax was explicit about the distinction that matters here: the Bank can look past a temporary gasoline jump but not persistent higher energy costs, and oil near 100 dollars would push inflation up in the coming months. A week of rising crude driven by six vessels struck near Hormuz on October 11 and traffic through the Strait at its lowest since July 23 is closer to the persistent case than the temporary one, and the 27% hike odds now have something behind them. What has not changed is the labour market, and it is why this is minus 1 rather than 0. September employment fell 68.3K against a plus 6.1K forecast after August's minus 41.7K - 110K of jobs in two months - and unemployment rose only to 6.5% from 6.4% because participation fell to 64.8%, the lowest since December 1997 outside 2020. August CPI is 3.0% headline with core at 2.4%, which is the number that stops the Bank moving this month, and annual growth is 1.1%. TD expects a hold through 2026 against markets carrying at least one hike before year-end. The Canadian ten-year is 3.8780%, among the lowest major yields on the board, and USD/CAD at 1.42681 is minus 0.09% on the day but plus 0.43% on the week after two consecutive weeks of being exactly flat - so the pair has finally started to pay the score gap just as the gap narrows. The consequence for the rest of the book is deliberate: USOIL flips to LONG today and Canada is upgraded in the same run, because a long crude thesis and a deeply short Canadian dollar cannot both be right. Macklem speaks tomorrow at 06:05 UTC and again October 16 at 04:15 UTC, straight into the energy question, and September CPI with the Survey of Consumer Expectations lands October 19."
