      read: "Two things firmed the dollar case today and one thing argues against raising it, which is why the score is held rather than moved. The first is pricing. December hike odds now run 82% to 90% across the sources checked this morning against the 65-75% range this report quoted yesterday, with October 28 still a hold at 18-21%. The driver is energy, not the committee: crude is plus 3.66% on the week and Brent plus 4.98% after six vessels were struck in and around Hormuz on October 11, and the commentary attributing the repricing to energy-driven inflation is explicit. The dollar is testing yearly highs on it, with DXY 102.422 minus 0.17% on the day but plus 0.58% on the week, plus 3.05% on the month and plus 4.17% year to date. The second is the dot plot, read one layer deeper than yesterday. The September projection table on federalreserve.gov has median funds midpoints of 4.1% for 2026 and 4.1% again for 2027, falling to 3.9% only in 2028 and 3.6% in 2029 against a 3.2% longer run, with PCE at 3.7% for 2026, core PCE at 3.4%, GDP at 2.3% and unemployment flat at 4.1% through 2029. Yesterday's file had all of that right but quoted the 2026 dispersion. The 2027 distribution is the more hawkish one and nobody is quoting it: 8 of 18 participants put 2027 at 4.375%, 6 at 4.125%, 3 at 3.625% and 1 at 3.125%. That is 14 of 18 at or above the median and nearly half the committee seeing two more quarter-point moves from the current 3.875% midpoint by the end of next year. One source note belongs here because it conflicts: TradingEconomics' own interest-rate page carries a projection row for the second year at 3.9%, which does not match the Fed's 2027 median of 4.1%, and the Fed's own publication governs its own projections. The argument against raising to plus 3 is the shape of the repricing. A hike priced off a supply shock in energy is a hike a committee can choose not to deliver, because looking through an oil-driven headline is the textbook response and Lagarde has already said she sees no evidence of energy feeding wages. The committee is deciding on core, and Wednesday at 12:30 UTC is the test: consensus is 0.2% m/m against a 0.20% Cleveland Fed nowcast, with the nowcast at about 2.39% y/y, while the headline the market is braced for at 3.6-3.7% y/y measures September and the rally that moved the odds happened after Friday. September was unanimous 12-0 with all 19 officials recorded as backing it in the October 7 minutes. The ten-year is 5.1980%, down about 3.9bp from yesterday, which is itself the small dissent: the long end is not confirming the hike repricing. September payrolls were 29K, unemployment 4.2%, August CPI 3.4% with core 2.4%. Chairman Warsh speaks as a red folder on October 16 at 03:30 UTC. Invalidated to plus 1 if core prints at or under 0.2% m/m."
