      read: "The euro was raised from minus 1 to 0 yesterday on three legs and the scoring discipline today is to say which one broke rather than to re-run the whole argument. Leg one, the Reuters poll conducted October 5 to 8, stands untouched: 70 of 73 economists expect the October 29 hold and 64 of 73 expect a quarter-point hike to 2.75% on December 17 when new projections arrive, and Aberdeen is publicly on the same call. TE re-verified the Deposit Facility Rate at 2.50%, in force since September 16, against a main refinancing rate of 2.65% and marginal lending at 2.90%. Leg three, the political timetable, also stands: open-session debate October 13-19, the revenue vote October 20, social security October 27, the whole text November 17, with the ordinance route unavailable until the 70-day limit expires on December 17 at midnight, and ING's current read is that the RN appears inclined to avoid censure rather than topple a government and leave 2027 without a budget, against Lecornu's roughly 54bn euros of consolidation. Leg two has reversed. OAT-Bund is 142.98bp this morning with the OAT at 4.9050% and the Bund at 3.4752%, against the 138.10bp quoted yesterday and the roughly 159bp of two Fridays ago - so the compression that justified half the upgrade has given back about 5bp, and it has done so on the exact morning the plenary debate opens. The price agrees with the widening rather than with the poll: EUR/USD is 1.11743, plus 0.17% on the day but minus 0.76% on the week - the weakest major on the week - minus 3.24% on the month and minus 4.82% year to date, which is worse than the minus 4.55% this report carried yesterday and keeps the euro the worst major of 2026. The bank channel that capped the upgrade is unchanged and still firing: Societe Generale, Credit Agricole and Deutsche Bank are each more than 15% below recent peaks and the Stoxx Europe 600 banks sector is roughly 10% off its August highs, with commentary noting the euro-area banks index reacts hardest when the France-Germany yield gap lurches - which is what it did today. The case for holding at 0 rather than cutting straight back to minus 1 is specific: the invalidation this report named yesterday was the bank correction extending or a censure motion carrying after October 20, and neither has happened, while the hardest forward number on the board still has 88% of a 73-economist panel expecting a December hike. Reversing a score twice inside forty-eight hours on a 5bp spread move would be flipping on noise. September HICP is 3.8% from 3.2% with core at 2.5% and unemployment 6.4%. Lagarde speaks twice tomorrow, at 02:10 and 08:30 UTC, and she is the test: she has kept to not pre-committing to a rate path and has said she sees no evidence at this stage of energy prices feeding into wages, which on a week of rising crude is the line to watch. Back to minus 1 if the spread keeps widening into the October 20 vote or the bank correction extends."
