      headline: "Held at -1 with a partial retraction: centralbank.watch does publish 88% for a hold on October 28, so the figure this report faded for three sessions was an odds-of-hold reading - and BNZ now expects an October hike with the OCR reaching 3.75%.",
      read: "Yesterday this report closed a four-session question by concluding that no probability exists for the October 28 meeting because New Zealand has no futures market tracking the Official Cash Rate directly. Half of that holds and half does not, and the half that does not needs retracting plainly. The instrument caveat is correct: the closest liquid contract settles on the 90-day bank bill rate rather than the OCR, so any published figure is a guide to direction rather than a precise readout. But centralbank.watch does publish a figure for this meeting - 88% for no change - which means the number this report faded for three sessions and then re-quoted yesterday was an odds-of-hold reading throughout, not an odds-of-hike reading, and the report had the polarity wrong rather than the number missing. With that fixed, the consensus and the market agree: Westpac expects a pause on October 28 before lifting the OCR to 3.00% in December, and TE's own forecast rows carry 2.75% for October 28 and 3.00% for December 9, in line with the Bank's September track. The new evidence runs hawkish and is why this stays at minus 1 rather than going back to minus 2. BNZ is now an outright outlier against that consensus, expecting a move at the October meeting and the OCR reaching 3.75% in time, and it notes that the trade-weighted index is at a 15-year low. Westpac has inflation finishing 2026 above 4% after a Q2 print of 4.1% against a 1-3% target, driven largely by higher fuel prices - which the week's oil rally makes worse rather than better, since crude is plus 3.66% and Brent plus 4.98%. A central bank facing above-target inflation with a fuel driver, a currency at a 15-year trade-weighted low and one house calling for an immediate move is not a two-point short. What keeps the score negative is everything backward-looking: core CPI at 3.2%, growth decelerating to plus 0.2% q/q, unemployment up to 5.6%, and the weakest data set in the G10 relative to a 2.75% policy rate. The curve still carries 56.7bp of tightening to February 17, which is two hikes - but that now sits against an RBA whose own minutes have reopened November, so the carry compression argument that cut AUD/NZD to LOW-MED yesterday has weakened and that long goes back to MEDIUM. NZD/USD is 0.55906, plus 0.05% on the day, minus 0.59% on the week and minus 3.26% on the month, and the ten-year is 4.9910%, down about 4.3bp. Q3 CPI on October 21 is the only thing that can force October; the Governor speaks October 15 and the general election is November 7. Back to minus 2 only if that CPI undershoots badly enough to take the February pricing out."
