      read: "Sterling keeps the upgrade it was restored to yesterday, and the correction today is to precision rather than direction - which matters, because yesterday's report replaced one over-confident number with another. It quoted November 5 as priced 'near 90%' in money markets. The range across sources this morning is 75% to 90%: one report carries roughly 90%, Polymarket shows 75.5% for a 25bp move, and centralbank.watch carries 89% on a page whose own metadata is months old and should be discounted. CIBC's Jeremy Stretch judges the market too deterministic in pricing a November hike, and Berenberg expects a hike while doubting it starts a cycle. So the move is widely expected but not the near-certainty yesterday implied, and the honest statement is a range. The committee leg is unchanged and is the firmest part of the case: the September 17 hold was 6-3 with Greene, Mann and Pill dissenting for 4.00%, Greene has now voted to hike at June, July and September, and Bank Rate has sat at 3.75% since December 2025. The energy leg strengthened over the weekend on the exact input Bailey named. UK gas printed 209.72 GBp/thm, plus 0.01% on the day but plus 11.22% on the week and plus 184.13% year to date, with EU gas at 84.21 EUR/MWh plus 11.25% on the week and plus 199.03% year to date. At the Istanbul Economic Forum on October 8 Bailey said the Bank's tolerance for energy-driven inflation is running out, that a look-through approach only works if inflation expectations stay anchored to target, that the October 28 Budget must be credible and be seen as credible by markets, and that we cannot in any way be relaxed about the current state of financial markets. A week in which gas rose 11% and crude rose 3.7% is that argument getting louder, not softer. The fiscal counterweight is why this is plus 1 and not plus 2 and none of it has improved: the Bank has paused gilt sales for six months and stopped sales of 20- and 30-year gilts entirely, the 30-year went through 6% around October 1 for the first time since early 1998, the Budget lands a week before the decision, and Thursday's August GDP is forecast at minus 0.1% m/m after plus 0.4%. The ten-year is 5.3918%, down about 2bp, still the highest major yield in the world at 19.38bp over the Treasury and 191.66bp over the Bund. August CPI is 3.1% with core 2.6%, wage growth 3.9% from 4.2% and unemployment 4.9%. Cable at 1.32176 is plus 0.07% on the day and minus 0.44% on the week, with 1.3140 the 2026 low holding support and 1.3274 then the September 30 peak at 1.3311 the levels to clear - and against a dollar that just repriced to 82-90% for December, the pair goes to a mild SHORT while the sterling crosses keep the upgrade. Bailey speaks October 14, 15, 16 and 17, the October 16 appearance at 02:00 UTC a red folder, and September CPI on October 21 is the decider."
