      read: "The yen's forward pricing has firmed materially and the score still does not move, which needs explaining rather than asserting. The new number is a swap-implied roughly 90% probability of another Bank of Japan hike by December, which is well above the 'most of one hike by December' futures reading this report has been quoting and is the firmest forward pricing the yen has carried in this file. October itself is unchanged and is a hold across every instrument: Kalshi carries 91-94% for maintaining the rate against about 6% for 25bp, futures-derived estimates give the October 30 hike about 26%, commentary read Ueda's post-September remarks as leaving virtually no chance of an October move, and former executive director Kazuo Momma puts consecutive hikes at 20-30% while calling a delay past January extremely unlikely. TE's own forecast row for October 30 is 1.25%. The policy rate is 1.25% after the 7-2 September 18 hike with Asada and Sato dissenting, the highest in 31 years. The structural story is confirmed at source today and is still under-appreciated. On October 8 Takaichi told the Diet that Japan's economy is no longer at a stage where it needs reflationary policies with monetary easing and fiscal expansion, adding that she has utmost respect for the autonomy of the BOJ, and her October 5 policy speech pledged to restrain JGB issuance by adjusting borrowing to tax revenues and debt-servicing costs with her food consumption tax cut funded without additional bonds. That is a supply story as much as a policy one, and it is why the thirty-year's record 4.235% print on October 5 and a ten-year at 3.0040% can sit alongside a government that arrived with a reflationist label. A Nomura economist's caution is the fair counterweight: the speech does not necessarily mean policy will actually be adjusted. So why hold at 0. The yen does not rally because the carry still pays and the dollar just got better: the Treasury-JGB gap is 219.40bp, narrower than yesterday's 222.10bp but still the dominant flow, and December Fed pricing moved to 82-90% in the same session the BoJ's December pricing firmed - so the differential has not compressed in expectation terms. USD/JPY at 158.308 is minus 0.19% on the day and plus 0.13% on the week, having again failed to sustain a break of the 158.50 area it has not held since late September, and the yen lost ground to nothing much either way across the G10. August CPI is 1.9% with core 1.7% and falling, unemployment 2.5% and wage growth 3.8% from 4.3% - a core inflation rate undershooting the target is the real argument against the yen, not the positioning. October 30 brings the Quarterly Outlook Report and its inflation forecast revision is the whole tradeable event; a hawkish revision with December already near 90% on swaps is what takes this score positive."
