      read: "The franc is quiet and what makes it worth reading today is what failed to happen rather than what did. The haven case was as strong as it gets this week - six commercial vessels struck in and around the Strait of Hormuz on October 11, Reuters counting seven transits on Tuesday against a typical 85 a day on one tracker's reckoning, the IRGC treating dark-mode shipping as hostile, and the US Navy striking Iranian-linked vessels - and the franc did nothing. EUR/CHF is 0.93096, minus 0.02% on the day and plus 0.13% year to date, holding above the 0.93 area Rabobank expects to hold. USD/CHF is 0.83318, minus 0.18% on the day but plus 0.18% on the week, plus 1.93% on the month and plus 5.08% year to date, so the dollar beat the franc again in a week built for the franc. That is the evidence for keeping the dollar legs long and the crosses at RANGE rather than reaching for a haven trade. The policy side gives no help in either direction: the SNB held at 0% on September 24 for a fifth consecutive assessment with the sight-deposit discount unchanged, CPI projections nudged up and the specific bias against rapid franc appreciation dropped from the statement, and on October 8 Vice Chairman Martin hinted at lower-for-longer while officials pushed back on near-term tightening. September CPI at 1.0% from 0.8% with core at 0.5% sits comfortably inside the 0-2% band, unemployment is 3.0%, annual growth 2.3% and the ten-year 0.5480%. The ceiling on any franc rally is unchanged and it is the central bank: Rabobank warns an accelerated bout of franc buying this autumn could force intervention, and BNP Paribas carries 0.94 at three months and 0.92 at twelve, which brackets spot from both sides. Intervention, not negative rates, remains the tool and the bar is a disorderly move rather than a directional one. The policy meeting minutes land October 22 and Chairman Schlegel speaks October 15 at 04:30 UTC; the next decision is December 10. The franc turns hawkish only if inflation keeps climbing off 1.0% while a haven bid finally appears, and this week is evidence that the second half of that condition is not being met."
