  geopolitics: "Yesterday this report ran what it called a clean experiment on the oil risk premium and drew the wrong conclusion from it, so the correction comes first. The argument was that the war had escalated into mass-casualty strikes on Saudi territory while crude fell 1.5% anyway, and that a market unwilling to pay for an attack on the largest Gulf producer was pricing the premium out. The market repriced it instead. WTI settled at 93.74 on Monday and Brent at 106.65, a gain of about 2% and Brent's highest since September 15, and this morning both give only a little back: WTI 92.715 at minus 1.09%, Brent 105.585 at minus 1.00%, leaving crude plus 3.66% on the week and Brent plus 4.98%. What this report under-weighted was the Strait rather than Saudi Arabia. Six commercial vessels - five tankers and a container ship - were struck in and around the Strait of Hormuz on October 11, and the pattern has changed shape: the IRGC has stepped up attacks on tankers transiting in dark mode along the southern corridor near the coast of Oman and has said that ships running with their identification systems off will be identified as hostile, while the US Navy is striking Iranian-linked vessels and has disabled a bulker. UKMTO's count has 41 of the 59 incidents involving commercial shipping since July 6 on that southern route, so the war has moved off the chokepoint itself and onto the workaround. Traffic has collapsed with it, and the figures conflict in a way worth stating rather than resolving. Reuters reported seven ships through on Tuesday and ten on Wednesday, the lowest since July 23. One tracker citing IMF PortWatch counts four vessels against a typical 85 a day and describes the Strait as effectively closed as of October 12. Those cover different days and different methods, and neither can see transponder-off traffic. What both agree on is the direction, and the offsetting fact is that Gulf producers are still moving enough crude around the Strait to keep regional exports near prewar levels - which is why this is a premium story rather than a physical-shortage story. On the Saudi side the escalation is real and the casualty figures still conflict. The Houthi strike on Riyadh's international airport on Saturday killed 13 and wounded more than 300 on Reuters' count, with another report giving 12 killed; Trump denounced the attacks after a call with the Saudi crown prince and a coalition intercepted missiles. Goldman Sachs raised its December Brent forecast to 90 dollars from 85 and puts the risk premium at just over 20 dollars a barrel - a forecast that is 15 dollars below spot Brent, which is a statement that they expect the premium to shrink rather than disappear. The policy counterweight is unchanged and still splits the energy complex. OFAC's General License 135, issued after the October 9 Trump-Putin call, authorises the sale, delivery, offloading and import of Russian-origin diesel through April 7 2027; it covers US restrictions only, with the EU and UK import bans and the product price cap still in force. US diesel futures fell 4% on the Friday and European diesel as much as 8.3%, and the cap is still visible in the relative prices: heating oil is plus 2.06% on the week and gasoline minus 0.74% against crude's plus 3.66%. Natural gas has no such waiver and no spare capacity, and it is where the damage is concentrated - EU gas 84.21 EUR/MWh at plus 11.25% on the week and plus 199.03% year to date, German gas 83.20 at plus 11.72% on the week, UK gas 209.72 GBp/thm at plus 11.22% on the week and plus 184.13% year to date. The FX reads follow and four of them changed today. First and largest, energy inflation has repriced the Fed: December hike odds moved from a 65-75% range to 82-90% across sources, with October 28 still a hold at 18-21%, and the dollar is testing yearly highs at DXY 102.422, plus 0.58% on the week and plus 4.17% year to date. That is the single transmission channel running through every pair in this book, and it is the reason the dollar is held at plus 2 rather than cut on a softening growth picture. Second, Canada comes off its low. The cut to minus 2 yesterday rested on WTI sitting exactly on the Bank's own baseline of crude falling toward 75 dollars by mid-2027; at 92.715 it is above that path and moving toward the 100 dollars Macklem said in Halifax would push inflation up in the coming months rather than the temporary gasoline jump he said the Bank can look through. CAD goes to minus 1 and USOIL flips from RANGE to LONG in the same run, deliberately - a long crude thesis and a two-point short in the largest oil-exporting major cannot both be right. Third, the equity indices turn. The DAX at 25,249 loses the Brent tailwind this report credited yesterday and gains a Brent headwind at plus 4.98% on the week, on top of German gas plus 4.96% on the day; GER40 flips from RANGE to SHORT. EU50 at 6,185 goes short on its own banking and sovereign weight, with OAT-Bund re-widening to 142.98bp from 138.10bp on the morning French plenary debate opens. Fourth, and this is the cleanest negative result of the week: there is no haven bid. Six ships struck, the Strait described by one tracker as effectively closed, and gold fell on Monday before recovering only as yields eased, while the franc did not move at all - EUR/CHF 0.93096 flat on the day, USD/CHF plus 5.08% year to date. In this regime an escalation is a dollar trade and an oil trade, not a gold or franc trade, and that is now evidence rather than assumption. The rolling tail is dated. Trump's pledge not to strike Iran expires with the midterms on November 3, and the administration's incentive to suppress fuel prices - of which General License 135 is the written form - reverses the day after.",
